  "worked_solution": "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=u(x){y}_{1}(x)=u{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u{e}^{-4x}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{\u2032} & ={u}^{\u2032}{e}^{-4x}-4u{e}^{-4x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{\u2033} & ={u}^{\u2033}{e}^{-4x}-8{u}^{\u2032}{e}^{-4x}+16u{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{\u2033}{e}^{-4x}-8{u}^{\u2032}{e}^{-4x}+16u{e}^{-4x})+8({u}^{\u2032}{e}^{-4x}-4u{e}^{-4x})+16u{e}^{-4x} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{\u2033}{e}^{-4x} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-4x}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{\u2033}=0.\n$$",
